.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt GET_MEMPOLICY 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm get_mempolicy
.Nd the memory policy of a thread or an address, on a machine of one memory node
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In numaif.h
.Ft long
.Fn get_mempolicy "int *mode" "unsigned long *nodemask" "unsigned long maxnode" "void *addr" "unsigned long flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn get_mempolicy
reports the policy
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
gave the calling thread, or the one
.Xr mbind 2
gave an address, and answers as Linux 6.12 answers on a machine with one
memory node, node 0.
libnuma's
.Fn numa_available
calls it to learn whether the kernel has the call at all.
.Pp
With
.Fa flags
0 and
.Fa addr
0 it stores the thread's mode, with its mode flags, in
.Fa mode
and the policy's nodes in
.Fa nodemask :
.Dv MPOL_DEFAULT
.Pq 0
and an empty mask for a thread without a policy, an empty mask for
.Dv MPOL_LOCAL ,
node 0 for a policy set without a mode flag, and for one set with a flag
the mask its caller gave.
With
.Dv MPOL_F_ADDR
.Pq 2
it answers the same for the policy of the mapping that holds
.Fa addr ,
or, in a mapping of a file in memory, of the file's page there; where
.Xr mbind 2
gave none the answer is
.Dv MPOL_DEFAULT ,
whatever the thread's policy.
.Dv MPOL_F_NODE
.Pq 1
with
.Dv MPOL_F_ADDR
stores instead of the mode the node of the page at
.Fa addr ,
0, bringing the page in for a read as a fault would.
.Dv MPOL_F_NODE
alone stores the node the thread allocates from next, 0, when its policy is
.Dv MPOL_INTERLEAVE
or
.Dv MPOL_WEIGHTED_INTERLEAVE .
.Dv MPOL_F_MEMS_ALLOWED
.Pq 4
stores 0 in
.Fa mode
and the nodes the thread may use, node 0, in
.Fa nodemask .
Either pointer may be null, and then is not written.
.Pp
The mask is written as Linux writes it: as many bytes as
.Fa maxnode No \- 1
bits take, rounded up to 64-bit words, the bytes past the first long
cleared.
A
.Fa maxnode
of 1 so has no byte written, except for a 32-bit program on the x86-64
kernel, which is always written its one 32-bit word.
.Ss System call interface
The system call number is 275 on i386 and 239 on x86-64.
On the x86-64 kernel a 32-bit program's mask is of 32-bit longs.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 on success.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/limitsprobe.c
reads the default policy with its empty mask, the allowed nodes, and the
node of a page of its own, and
.Pa tests/numaprobe.c
reads back each policy it sets.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa mode
or
.Fa nodemask
cannot be written;
.Dv MPOL_F_ADDR
names an address in no mapping; or the page whose node
.Dv MPOL_F_NODE
asks for cannot be read, in a mapping without read access, a guard
region, device memory or secret memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa nodemask
is given with a
.Fa maxnode
of 0; the mask would take more than a page;
.Fa flags
has a bit Linux does not define, or
.Dv MPOL_F_MEMS_ALLOWED
with another flag;
.Fa addr
is not 0 without
.Dv MPOL_F_ADDR ;
or
.Dv MPOL_F_NODE
comes without
.Dv MPOL_F_ADDR
in a thread whose policy does not interleave.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mbind 2 ,
.Xr move_pages 2 ,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2 ,
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
